<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<Description>A fluent Given-When-Then specification framework for xUnit. Write behavior definitions as unit tests with built-in auto-mocking and data generation</Description>
		<VersionPrefix></VersionPrefix>
		<Authors>Joakim Sigvald</Authors>
		<TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
		<AssemblyName>TSpec</AssemblyName>
		<RootNamespace>TSpec</RootNamespace>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<PackageTags>unit test specification given when then fluent mock xunit automock</PackageTags>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/joakimsigvald/TSpec</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/joakimsigvald/TSpec.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageVersion>3.0.0</PackageVersion>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>
			REMOVED
			* Given&lt;T&gt;(setup) and Given&lt;T&gt;(transform); use Using&lt;T&gt;
			* Then().DoesNotThrow(); use Then().Completes()
			* Another&lt;T&gt;(); use Any&lt;T&gt;()
			* The wasInvoked overloads taking Moq's Times; use TSpec.Times
			CHANGED
			* Requires xUnit v3 4.x: installing TSpec 3.0 upgrades a test project's xunit.v3 to 4.0.1 or later, with xUnit 4's own breaking changes
			* TSpec mocks with its own engine on Castle.Core and no longer depends on Moq: reference Moq directly if your tests use it. A TSpec mock is not a Moq mock, so Mock.Get on one throws; a mock made with Moq can be handed to the subject with Using
			* A throw on an awaited mocked call (Task, Task&lt;T&gt;, ValueTask, ValueTask&lt;T&gt;) comes back as a faulted task, however the throw was set up
			* A failed verification by expression fails like a count by name, e.g. "Expected IOrderService.CreateOrder(the ShoppingCart) to be invoked once but was never invoked", instead of throwing Moq's MockException
			* A failed verification lists the calls the mock received, in order, each with its arguments
			* A count not met reads "was never invoked" or "was invoked once" instead of "was invoked 0 times" or "1 times"
			* A mock renders as the type it mocks, e.g. IOrderService, instead of Mock&lt;IOrderService:1&gt;.Object
			* Setting up or verifying a non-virtual member throws SetupFailed saying why
			* Moq's It.IsAny or It.Is inside a TSpec setup or verification throws SetupFailed; write Any&lt;T&gt;() or Any&lt;T&gt;(constraint)
			* Any&lt;T&gt;() or Any&lt;T&gt;(constraint) inside an argument rather than as one, e.g. new Filter { Id = Any&lt;int&gt;() }, throws SetupFailed. It never meant any value: on 2.8 it matched only the type's default
			* An argument that reads the mock itself, e.g. _ =&gt; _.Get(_.Id), throws SetupFailed saying what to write instead, rather than an InvalidOperationException
			* A call set up or verified through a chain, e.g. _.Orders(2).Find(1), reaches a mock per step and arguments; a call on it the chain did not set up answers as Given&lt;IOrderStore&gt;() set it up
			* A chain through an awaited member, e.g. _.GetCustomerAsync(1).Result.Name, reads without .Result in the specification
			* An unset member returning Task&lt;T&gt; or ValueTask&lt;T&gt; of an interface answers as a member returning T does, instead of throwing "Interface types returned as task must be provided explicitly"
			FIXED
			* A mocked call chained through a method with arguments reads as written, e.g. IParent.GetChild(2).Get(1), instead of "get child 2"
			* A call to a mocked delegate reads as the delegate invoked, e.g. "Given TryLookup(1, out _found) returns true" and "Then Func&lt;int, string&gt;(1)", instead of "TryLookup._(1, out _found)" and "Func&lt;int, string&gt;. 1"
			* A tap before First() taps every call of the sequence and is stated in the specification; it was dropped from both
			* A sequence on a call with Any&lt;T&gt;() reads "Get(any int) first" in the specification instead of "Get(Any&lt;int&gt;()) first"
			* Throws&lt;E&gt;() on a Task&lt;T&gt; call threw ArgumentNullException instead of E
			* Throws on a protected member returning Task or ValueTask failed setup
			* ReturnsDefault() on a Task call answered with a null task; it now answers with a completed one
			* A Task call past the last step of its sequence answered with a null task; it now answers with a completed one
			* Returns() on a call that answers with a value names the value's type in its refusal, not a Moq class
			* Setup failures about a mock's default answers name generic types as C# writes them, e.g. ICollection&lt;int&gt; rather than ICollection`1
		</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Castle.Core" Version="5.2.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="4.0.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="xunit.v3.assert" Version="4.0.1" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\TSpec-agent-reference.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
